```{r, include = FALSE}
knitr::opts_chunk$set(collapse = TRUE, comment = "#>", error = FALSE)
```

In real studies, some data are almost always missing: a patient did not
complete a questionnaire, a blood sample was lost, etc. In this tutorial, you
will learn to:

1. Understand why the simple solutions to missing data are usually bad ideas.
2. Impute the missing values multiple times with `impute.glmnet.matrix_fit`
   and `impute.glmnet.matrix`.
3. Include the imputation in the cross-validation correctly.
4. Interpret the models and predict new patients when there are several models
   per fold (one per imputation).

We assume that you have read the first two tutorials
([Get started](easy.glmnet.html) and [Evaluating and interpreting your model](evaluating-and-interpreting.html)).

This tutorial takes about 20–30 minutes. Imputing takes some time, so, unlike
in the previous tutorials, the complete code of this tutorial takes a few
minutes to run.


## The data

We use the same data as in the previous tutorials, with the 40 numeric
variables. But now, let's imagine that two of the variables, `X21` and `X39`,
could not be recorded in 30% of the patients:

```{r}
library(easy.glmnet)
x = as.matrix(patients[, paste0("X", 1:40)])
y = patients$relapse
x_new = as.matrix(new_patients[, paste0("X", 1:40)])
y_new = new_patients$relapse

set.seed(1)
for (v in c("X21", "X39")) {
  x[sample(nrow(x), 0.3 * nrow(x)), v] = NA
  x_new[sample(nrow(x_new), 0.3 * nrow(x_new)), v] = NA
}
```

In R, missing values are represented by `NA` ("not available"):

```{r}
x[1:6, c("X20", "X21", "X22", "X39")]
```

Note that we also removed values in the new patients: in real life, future
patients will also have missing data, and our model must be able to predict
them too.


## Step 1: The simple solutions

`glmnet_fit` cannot use data with missing values, so we have to do something.
There are two simple solutions, but both are usually bad ideas.

### Removing the patients with missing values

We could use only the patients with complete data:

```{r}
complete = complete.cases(x)
sum(complete)
```

We would lose `r 100 - sum(complete)` of our 100 patients, and with them, a lot
of information. Moreover, the model could not predict the future patients with
missing values, i.e., `r round(100 * mean(!complete.cases(x_new)))`% of our
new patients.

### Removing the variables with missing values

We could also remove `X21` and `X39`. But these were the two most relevant
variables in the previous tutorial! Removing them would waste all the
information that they have in the 70% of patients in whom they were recorded.

### Imputing the missing values

The better solution is to **impute** the missing values, i.e., to replace them
with plausible values estimated from the other variables. For instance, if
`X21` is associated with other variables, we can estimate the value of `X21`
of a patient from the values of these other variables.

Note, however, that simply replacing each missing value with its prediction
(e.g., with `predict`) is not enough. A prediction is the expected value for a
patient, so all the imputed values would lie exactly on the regression line:
they would vary less than real values, and they would be more strongly
associated with the other variables than real values are. Thus, a good
imputation must add random noise of the right size to the predictions, so that
the imputed values vary as much as real values would.


## Step 2: Multiple imputation

### How it works

`impute.glmnet.matrix_fit` fits, for each variable, lasso models that estimate
it from the other variables (Solanes et al., 2022; Palau et al., 2023; Salazar
de Pablo et al., 2025). It does it even for the variables without missing
values, because future patients may have missing values in any variable. Then,
`impute.glmnet.matrix` uses these models to replace the missing values.

As explained above, `impute.glmnet.matrix` does not simply use the
predictions of these models: it adds random noise with the size of the
prediction error of each imputation model (for binary variables, it randomly
assigns 0 or 1 according to the predicted probability). Moreover, an imputed
value is only an estimate, and pretending that we know it exactly would make
us overconfident. For this reason, `impute.glmnet.matrix` imputes the values
several times, creating several complete datasets, each with slightly
different imputed values. This is called **multiple imputation**. Then, we will fit a model with
each imputed dataset, and average their predictions.

### Doing it

As with `data.frame2glmnet.matrix` in the first tutorial, there are two
functions: one that learns how to impute (`impute.glmnet.matrix_fit`) and one
that applies the imputation (`impute.glmnet.matrix`). Let's see what they do
with all our patients:

```{r}
m_impute = impute.glmnet.matrix_fit(x, verbose = FALSE)
x_imputed = impute.glmnet.matrix(m_impute, x, nimp = 5, verbose = FALSE)
```

`nimp` is the number of imputations. `x_imputed` is a list of 5 complete
matrices. For example, these are the first rows of the first two imputed
datasets:

```{r}
x_imputed[[1]][1:6, c("X20", "X21", "X22", "X39")]
x_imputed[[2]][1:6, c("X20", "X21", "X22", "X39")]
```

The observed values are the same in all the imputed datasets, whereas the
imputed values (those that were `NA`) differ slightly. In this tutorial, we
use 5 imputations so that the code runs faster, but in a real study, we
recommend using more (the default is 20).

Note that we only imputed the predictors, never the outcome: `y` was not
included in `x`. In the new patients, the outcome is what we want to predict,
so it cannot be used to impute anything. Similarly, patients whose outcome is
missing cannot be used to build the model, so they must be excluded (with
survival outcomes, patients lost to follow-up are instead censored, as you will
see in the last tutorial).


## Step 3: Imputation within the cross-validation

### Lesson 2 again

Imputing learns from the data: `impute.glmnet.matrix_fit` finds the models to
estimate the missing values, and it learns these models from the patients we
give it. Thus, following Lesson 2 of the first tutorial, the imputation must be
inside `fit_fun`, using only the training patients. Then, `predict_fun`
imputes the test patients with the models learned from the training patients,
exactly as we would do with a future patient.

### The code

Now, the model has two parts: the imputation models, and one lasso model for
each imputed dataset. `fit_fun` returns both in a `list`:

```{r}
nimp = 5
fit_fun = function (x_training, y_training) {
  m = list(
    impute = impute.glmnet.matrix_fit(x_training, verbose = FALSE),
    lasso = list()
  )
  x_imputed = impute.glmnet.matrix(m$impute, x_training, nimp = nimp, verbose = FALSE)
  for (i in 1:nimp) {
    m$lasso[[i]] = glmnet_fit(x_imputed[[i]], y_training, family = "binomial")
  }
  m
}
```

`predict_fun` imputes the test patients, applies each lasso model to its
imputed dataset, and averages the predictions:

```{r}
predict_fun = function (m, x_test) {
  x_imputed = impute.glmnet.matrix(m$impute, x_test, nimp = nimp, verbose = FALSE)
  y.pred = NULL
  for (i in 1:nimp) {
    y.pred = cbind(y.pred, predict(m$lasso[[i]], x_imputed[[i]]))
  }
  rowMeans(y.pred)
}
```

(`cbind` puts the predictions of the different imputations side by side, as
columns of a matrix, and `rowMeans` averages each row, i.e., each patient.)

Now we can run the cross-validation as usual. This is the step that takes a
few minutes:

```{r, warning = FALSE}
res = cv(x, y, family = "binomial", fit_fun, predict_fun, verbose = FALSE)
res
```

(We hid the warning about `type.measure`, which you can ignore as explained in
the first tutorial.)

Despite the missing values, the cross-validation estimates an AUC similar to
the one we obtained in the previous tutorial with complete data.

The calibration slope, however, is higher than 1, i.e., the estimated
probabilities are somewhat too modest. This may be because the imputed values of
`X21` and `X39` are estimated from the other variables only, so they are less
associated with relapse than the real values would be, and the model relies a
bit less on these variables than it should. Keep in mind, though, that with 100
patients, the calibration slope is quite imprecise (see the second tutorial for
how to interpret it).


## Step 4: Interpretation and new patients

### Several models per fold

In the previous tutorial, each fold had one lasso model. Now, each fold has
5 lasso models (one per imputation), stored in the `lasso` element of the list
returned by `fit_fun`. The functions to interpret the models accept this
structure: we only have to tell them the name of this element with
`childname`:

```{r}
count = glmnet_get.items.count(res$models, "lasso")
count[which(count >= 0.9)]
relevance = glmnet_get.items.relevance(res$models, "lasso")
round(relevance[which(relevance >= 0.05)], 3)
```

Here, the count and the relevance are calculated across the
`r length(res$models)` folds × `r nimp` imputations =
`r length(res$models) * nimp` models.

### The model to use with new patients

As in the previous tutorial, there are two options. The first option is to
build the model with all the patients, using `fit_fun`. The result includes
the imputation and the lasso models, so `predict_fun` can apply it to
new patients, including those with missing values:

```{r, warning = FALSE}
m_all = fit_fun(x, y)
y.pred_all = predict_fun(m_all, x_new)
cbind(simple.accuracy(y_new, y.pred_all, "auc"),
      simple.calibration(y_new, y.pred_all, "binomial"))
```

The AUC in the new patients is in line with the
cross-validation estimate, and the calibration slope is also higher than 1.
The calibration-in-the-large is positive for the reason explained in the
previous tutorial: fewer of our 100 patients relapsed than of the new
patients.

The second option is to choose the most representative lasso model with
`glmnet_get.main.model`:

```{r}
m_main = glmnet_get.main.model(res$models, "lasso", verbose = FALSE)
m_main
```

This model is useful to report and interpret the model (a single formula instead
of `r length(res$models) * nimp` lasso models). However, to apply it to new
patients with missing values, we would also need imputation models, so use the
first option to predict new patients.


## Summary

- Removing the patients or the variables with missing values wastes
  information, and removing the patients also prevents predicting future
  patients with missing values.
- `impute.glmnet.matrix_fit` learns how to impute the missing values, and
  `impute.glmnet.matrix` imputes them multiple times (`nimp`).
- Imputing learns from the data, so it must be inside `fit_fun` (Lesson 2).
  Fit one model per imputed dataset, and average their predictions in
  `predict_fun`.
- Do not use the outcome to impute the predictors: future patients will not
  have it.
- With several models per fold, use `childname` in
  `glmnet_get.items.count`, `glmnet_get.items.relevance`, and
  `glmnet_get.main.model`.

In the next tutorial, [Continuous and survival outcomes, regression to the mean, and multisite data](other-outcomes-and-sites.html)
(`vignette("other-outcomes-and-sites")`), you will learn to predict continuous and
survival outcomes, and to deal with data from several sites.


